\section{Interface}{

With \code{dateHandling = NULL}, the column named by \code{year} supplies exact calendar
years. Interval mode uses a named list such as:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{list(
  start = "startDate",
  end = "endDate",
  ambiguous = "maxOverlap",
  endOnly = "drop",
  startOnly = "drop",
  ties = "drop"
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{start} and \code{end} name the earliest and latest possible observation-date
columns. Values may be numeric calendar years, \code{Date} values, four-digit year
strings, or ISO \code{YYYY-MM-DD} strings. Malformed values, impossible dates, and
starts after ends are errors. In interval mode, \code{year = NULL} is permitted.
If both bounds are missing, a usable exact year is the fallback. If either
bound is present, the interval information takes precedence over \code{year}.
}

\section{Assignment rules}{

A bounded interval is assigned automatically when every calendar year it
touches is contained in one supplied period. Gaps in a period therefore
prevent automatic containment. Every retained record is assigned to at most
one period.

For other bounded intervals, \code{ambiguous} is one of:
\itemize{
\item \code{"drop"} (default): exclude the record.
\item \code{"start"}: use the period containing the earliest possible date.
\item \code{"end"}: use the period containing the latest possible date.
\item \code{"maxOverlap"}: use the period with greatest overlap. Year-only bounds use
overlapping calendar-year counts; full dates use inclusive days within the
actual years listed in each period.
}

Maximum-overlap ties use \code{ties = "drop"} (default), \code{"earliest"}, or
\code{"latest"}. Earliest/latest choices are chronological and do not depend on
list order. A bounded interval with no overlap is excluded.

An end-only record means "on or before" its bound. \code{endOnly = "drop"} is the
default; \code{"earliest"} assigns the chronologically earliest compatible period.
A start-only record means "on or after" its bound. \code{startOnly = "drop"} is the
default; \code{"latest"} assigns the chronologically latest compatible period.
These allocations are analyst assumptions, not information contained in the
record.
}

\section{Interpretation and diagnostics}{

Interval allocation does not estimate the true date, duplicate records,
weight them fractionally, or imply continuous observation throughout a date
range. \code{maxOverlap} is a pragmatic allocation rule, not a probability model.
Preferential early or late allocation can change apparent temporal trends.

Results retain their established named components. A
\code{temporalDiagnostics} attribute reports unambiguous and rule-based
assignments, exclusions by reason, unresolved ties, open-ended decisions,
outside-window records, missing temporal information, and final counts per
supplied period. Material interval exclusions or assumption-based allocations
produce one summary warning rather than one warning per record.

Darwin Core \code{eventDate} can contain ISO dates or intervals. This interface
does not parse the complete Darwin Core interval syntax directly: split
bounded values into start and end columns first, retaining missing bounds for
open-ended records.
}

